from: planner (ta-anchor-dynamic-refresh Type-3 owner)
to: review (PR-2 internal code reviewer / Type-1 owner)
session: ta-anchor — claim pricing vs. spend pricing under GPUsPerReplica asymmetry

## Why this is yours

It contradicts a rationale stated twice in landed code and traceable to the frozen Type 1's GPU-space
pivot. Per Dean's standing routing, a Type-1 problem goes to you, not into a Type-3 edit. I am not
asking for a code change in C6f and I have made none.

## The finding

A role's fair-share **claim** is priced through `referenceVariantForRole`; the entitlement is **spent**
through whichever variant the picker lands on. Both convert between the analyzer's metric and GPUs,
but through **different variants' `GPUsPerReplica`**:

- `claimGPUs` (`greedy_score_optimizer.go:85`) — `gpusPR := gpusPerReplicaFromState(stateMap, vc.VariantName)`
  where `vc` is `referenceVariantForRole(variants, role)`, then `toGPUs(ps[i][role], prcForVariant(...), gpusPR)`.
- `fairShareRolePick` (`:678-684`) — `capN := replicasToCover(share, gpusPR)` with `gpusPR` read from the
  **candidate this loop landed on**.

So `claim = demand/PRC_ref × gpusPR_ref` and `spend = n × gpusPR_picked`. Equal only when
`gpusPR_ref == gpusPR_picked`. When the reference variant is the more GPU-hungry one, the claim — and
therefore the model's entitlement — is inflated by `gpusPR_ref / gpusPR_picked`.

## Why the existing rationale does not cover it

`referenceVariantForRole`'s doc comment (`:830-838`) anticipates reference ≠ picked and dismisses it:

> That disagreement needs no correction in GPU space: the cap divides by whichever candidate the
> picker landed on, and GPUs per replica is immutable deployment topology rather than a re-derived
> capacity.
>
> Pricing a whole role through one representative variant is an approximation whenever the role's
> variants have unequal **per-replica capacities**. It is pre-existing, and denominating in GPUs
> neither introduces nor removes it.

`fairShareRolePick:678-681` repeats the first half ("there is no reference capacity to compensate for").

The named approximation is **PRC** asymmetry. The defect is **`GPUsPerReplica`** asymmetry — the
quantity the same comment offers as the reason the divergence is safe, on the grounds that it is
immutable topology. Immutability is not the property needed: what is needed is that the two
conversions use the *same* value, and they do not. The dismissal is sound for PRC and does not
transfer.

## Evidence

The coder's C6f reproducer (`plan__ta-anchor-c6f-w4-no-spend-is-false.md`, measured):
`cheap-v` = reference, `PRC 10000`, `GPUsPerReplica 3`, at its `MaxReplicas 1` ceiling;
`pricey-v` = picked, `PRC 10000`, `GPUsPerReplica 1`. sat demand 30000, pool 100 (never binds),
single model so `allocationMean = 0` and `target == claim`.

`claim = 30000/10000 × 3 = 9` GPUs. sat's demand will actually be served at 1 GPU/replica, i.e. 3 GPUs.
The claim is inflated exactly 3×, which is `gpusPR_ref / gpusPR_picked`.

This also answers the mechanism question he left open. His regime D (P/D, every `GPUsPerReplica = 1`)
holds because that configuration removes the asymmetry, so there is no inflation to expose — not
because of the cross-role `deltaUtil` min or the named-role `roleAggRemaining` path, his two
candidates. The requirement is `gpusPR_ref ≠ gpusPR_picked`, which is what he observed empirically
without a mechanism.

## Two consequences, one measured and one not

1. **Measured (his case C).** With the inflated entitlement in place, an entry that makes no claim —
   because it cannot price the reference variant — is also not clamped by it, and votes past the
   claiming entry's bottleneck to fill the inflated entitlement. `+3` replicas becomes `+9`. This is
   the `W4` no-spend violation, but the inflation is upstream of `W4`: `W4`'s escape is what *reveals*
   it, not what causes it.
2. **Analytically implied, NOT measured — flagging as unverified.** A single analyzer is protected by
   `n = min(bottleneck, capN)`, so an inflated entitlement alone does not over-spend. But the claim
   is also the model's **ranking and entitlement quantity** in a multi-model pass, so an inflated
   claim should win a larger share against other models with no second analyzer involved. I have not
   built that fixture and am not asserting it. It is cheap to test on the harness the coder already
   has, and it is the question that decides whether this is a corner or a fair-share distortion.

## What I am not claiming

- No view that the GPU-space pivot itself is wrong. The pivot is what makes the two conversions
  *comparable enough to notice*; in replica space the mismatch was hidden inside `fairShareCap`.
- Not asserting any real accelerator catalog produces the asymmetry. The coder says the same and
  notes nothing validates against it either.
- No `W4` disposition. C6f landed test-only-and-scoped per Dean's standing instruction, with the
  case-C numbers in the fixture comment; that stands whatever you conclude here.

## One note on the fork the coder offered, since it bears on your answer

His option 2 has two variants. The second — "the clamp keys on the picked variant so clamp and vote
agree by construction" — is not implementable as written: the clamp runs at
`allocateForModel:440-469`, before the pick at `:471+`, so there is no picked variant yet. The first
variant (bar the entry from `votesFromPickerState` for variants whose *role reference* it cannot
price) would make `W4` true, but it gates a vote for a variant the entry **can** price on a fact about
a different variant — importing the reference approximation into the vote path. Neither is a clean
fix, which is consistent with the cause being the claim pricing rather than the abstention.

